 {"id":"RELEASE-OB-019","title":"[P1] Toolchain floor: go.mod pins go 1.26.9, which carries GO-2026-6809 (crypto/x509 compromised intermediate CA); v0.3.0 assets were built on 1.26.10 but host/dev builds still use 1.26.9","status":"complete","priority":"P1","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-04T16:54:13Z","updated_at":"2026-11-16T19:57:38Z","capability_tags":[],"reasoning":"govulncheck ./... at 57bca46c (v0.3.0 base) reports exactly one finding: GO-2026-6809, Distrust compromised intermediate certificates in crypto/x509, Found in crypto/x509@go1.26.9, Fixed in crypto/x509@go1.26.10, with a reachable trace cmd/off-by-one/main.go:366:30 off.main -> http.Server.ListenAndServe -> x509.Certificate.Verify. Standard-library findings belong to the artifact-build owner, not the dependency owner: no module in go.mod is implicated. Host toolchain: go version go1.26.9 linux/amd64, go env GOTOOLCHAIN=auto, GOVERSION=go1.26.9, go.mod line 3 says go 1.26.9. The v0.3.0 release assets were deliberately cross-built with GOTOOLCHAIN=go1.26.10 (already in the module cache at ~/go/pkg/mod/golang.org/toolchain@v0.0.1-go1.26.10.linux-amd64) so users are not exposed, but every local make build, every make release run without the env prefix, and the deployed service binary still link the vulnerable stdlib.","acceptance_criteria":"Raise the Go toolchain floor in go.mod to 1.26.11 (supersedes the row's original 1.26.10 target: 1.26.10 closes GO-2026-6809 but GO-2026-6859 net/http needs 1.26.11, so govulncheck zero is only reachable at 1.26.11) and pin every ci.yml setup-go input to the same exact patch so CI and local builds share one stdlib; document the GOTOOLCHAIN prefix in the release procedure. Prove: govulncheck ./... reports no vulnerabilities at the new HEAD; a bare make build links go1.26.11 (go version -m); make test green; go vet clean; go.sum unchanged; every setup-go pin reads 1.26.11; cite the green CI run id.","detail":{"fingerprint":"8202a6fb92f50c2c5d3cbdb3122ffdb3a4294ef0e